606 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1168 0 R
/E (The FORD framework utilizes fog computing by deploying computational tasks to nearby fog nodes and cloud resources. It applies latency-aware resource scheduling to assign tasks to the closest available nodes, minimizing execution time. A fault-tolerant mechanism ensures that tasks that fail at one node are rescheduled elsewhere to guarantee completion, with data securely stored in the cloud.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
607 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1169 0 R
/E (This framework reduces latency by processing data closer to its source and balances workload between fog and cloud for efficiency. It enhances reliability through fault tolerance, preventing task failures from impacting system performance. The approach supports high data integrity and seamless integration of fog and cloud, making it suited for time-sensitive smart city applications.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
608 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1170 0 R
/E (Challenges include the complexity of dynamically scheduling tasks across heterogeneous fog and cloud nodes. Fault tolerance mechanisms add overhead and may increase communication costs. Ensuring security and privacy throughout distributed layers is non-trivial, especially when managing massive sensor-generated data in real time.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
